<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malbork</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Marienburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malbork (German Marienburg) is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogat">Nogat</a> River in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, built around the castle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, its headquarters from 1309 to 1457. The castle is the largest brick Gothic fortress in the world and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Town and castle passed to Poland in 1457, during the Thirteen Years' War.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malbork.jpg" alt="Malbork" width="320" height="259" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castillo_de_Malbork,_Polonia,_2013-05-19,_DD_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Malbork lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogat">Nogat</a> river in the region of Powiśle, in the Pomeranian Voivodeship of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The river and the flat terrain allowed barges to reach the town a hundred kilometres from the sea. The town grew around the fortress Ordensburg Marienburg, founded in 1274 on the east bank of the Nogat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>. Both castle and town were named for their patron saint, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>, and are called Marienburg in German and Malbork in Polish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> the town collected tolls on river traffic and the Order held a monopoly on the amber trade. The town later joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Battle of Grunwald</a> the Order was greatly weakened, and the Teutonic Knights burned the town in 1410 before the Polish and Lithuanian siege of the castle, which failed. In the Thirteen Years' War the Order pawned the castle to its soldiers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, who sold it in 1457 to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV of Poland</a>. The town resisted for three more years under Mayor Bartholomäus Blume, who was hanged and quartered when it fell.</p><p>From 1457 to 1772 the castle was one of the royal residences of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and from 1568 it housed the Polish Admiralty. In 1710, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>, half the population died of cholera. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> brought the town to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1772. Near the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the town was declared a fortress and almost completely destroyed in fighting between German forces and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, which lasted until 9 March 1945. In June 1945 it was handed to Polish authorities and renamed Malbork, and the remaining German population was expelled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork-castle">Malbork Castle</a> was built over nearly 230 years and is in fact three castles combined in one. It is the world's largest brick castle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s largest Gothic fortress. More than half of it was destroyed in the war, and restoration has been ongoing since. The castle and its museum are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Other sights include the Gothic church of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>, the Gothic town hall and parts of the medieval town walls.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malbork” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was divided into voivodeships on the Polish model: after 1466 Pomeranian, Chełmno and Marienburg (<b>Malbork</b>).</blockquote><a href="/empire/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its brick Gothic architecture, above all the castle of Marienburg (<b>Malbork</b>), which grew into one of the largest brick fortresses in the world and is now a UNESCO World Heritage Site, shaped building across the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After two months, King Władysław II Jagiełło and Grand Duke Vytautas lifted their siege of Marienburg (<b>Malbork</b>), capital of the Teutonic Knights, meant to complete the conquest of Prussia after their victory at Grunwald.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-19">19 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
